<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-radical" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Radical of a module</title>
    
    
    <introduction>
        <p>
            We assume that <m>R</m> is a commutative ring with unity, and that <m>A</m> is a right <m>R</m>-algebra. Consider <m>M</m> as a right <m>A</m>-module.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <definition xml:id="def-radical-of-a-module">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The <term>radical</term> of a right <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m> is
                <me>
                    \rad{M}=\bigcap_{\substack{N\leq M\\M/N\text{ is simple}}}N
                </me>
                If <m>M</m> has no <m>A</m>-submodule <m>N</m> such that <m>M/N</m> is simple then, we write <m>\rad{M}=M</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <example xml:id="ex-radical-is-whole-module">
        <title>Radical of <m>\Q</m> as a <m>\Z</m>-module is <m>\Q</m></title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider <m>\Q</m> as a <m>\Z</m>-module. Suppose there is a proper submodule <m>N</m> of <m>\Q</m> such that <m>\Q/N</m> is simple. By <xref ref="prop-equivalent-criterion-for-simple-modules"/>, there exists a prime number <m>p</m> such that <m>\Q/N\simeq\Z/p\Z</m> as <m>\Z</m>-modules. Thus, for any <m>x\in\Q</m>, <m>px\in N</m>. Choose <m>x\in\Q\setminus N</m>. Then there is <m>y\in\Q</m> such that <m>py=x</m>. Note that <m>x=py\in N</m>, a contradiction. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>
    <p>
        We collect a few observations related to radical in the following lemma.
    </p>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-observations-related-to-radical">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>M</m> be a right <m>A</m>-module.
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The radical of <m>M</m> is an <m>A</m>-submodule of <m>M</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If <m>N\leq M</m> and <m>\rad{(M/N)}=0</m> then, <m>\rad{M}\subseteq N</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The radical of <m>M/\rad{M}</m> is zero.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-radical-of-semisimple-module-is-trivial">
        <statement>
            <p>
                If a right <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m> is semisimple then, <m>\rad{M}=0</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>
    <proof>
        <p>
            Let <m>M=\oplus_{i\in I}N_i</m>, where <m>N_i</m> is a simple <m>A</m>-module. For every <m>i\in I</m> consider <m>L_i=\oplus_{k\neq i}N_k</m>. Then, <m>M/L_i\simeq N_i</m> is simple for each <m>i\in I</m>. Thus, <m>\rad{M}\subseteq\cap_iL_i=\{0\}</m>.
        </p>
    </proof>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-artinian-and-radical-zero-iff-finitely-generated-semisimple">
        <statement>
            <p>
                A right <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m> is finitely generated and semisimple if and only if <m>M</m> is Artinian and <m>\rad{M}=0</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>
    <proof>
        <p>
            Suppose that <m>M</m> is a finitely generated and semisimple <m>A</m>-module. By <xref ref="thm-semisimple-artinian-noetherian-modules"/> and <xref ref="lem-radical-of-semisimple-module-is-trivial"/>, the module <m>M</m> is Artinian and its radical is trivial.
        </p>
        <p>
            Conversely, assume that <m>M</m> is Artinian and that <m>\rad{M}=0</m>. Suppose that <m>\rad{M}</m> is the inversection of <m>N_i</m> (<m>i\in I</m>) such that <m>M/N_i</m> is simple. Note that as <m>\rad{M}=0</m>, the set <m>I</m> is not empty. Consider the following collection of <m>A</m>-submodules of <m>M</m>
            <me>
                \Sigma=\{N_{i_1}\cap\cdots\cap N_{i_k}:i_1,\ldots,i_k\in I\}
            </me>
            This is a nonempty collection of submodules of an Artinian module, hence, by <xref ref="lem-existence-of-minimal-maximal-element"/>, the collection <m>\Sigma</m> has a minimal element, say <m>N_1\cap\cdots\cap N_r</m>. We first observe that this minimal element is zero. Indeed, if <m>N_1\cap\cdots\cap N_r\neq 0</m> then for some <m>i\in I</m>, <m>N_1\cap\cdots\cap N_r</m> is not a subset of <m>N_i</m> and hence, <m>N_1\cap\cdots\cap N_r\cap N_i\subsetneq N_1\cap\cdots\cap N_r</m>, a contradiction to minimality. Therefore, <m>N_1\cap\cdots\cap N_r=0</m>. We define an <m>A</m>-module homomorphism <m>\varphi\colon M\to\oplus_{i=1}^rM/N_i</m> by 
            <me>
                m\mapsto(m+N_1,\ldots,m+N_r).
            </me>
            This is a surjective map with the kernel <m>N_1\cap\cdots\cap N_r</m>. Hence, 
            <me>
                M\simeq (M/N_1)\oplus\cdots\oplus(M/N_r).
            </me>
            This shows that <m>M</m> is semisimple. It is also finitely generated because <m>M</m> given to be Artinian (see <xref ref="thm-semisimple-artinian-noetherian-modules"/>).
        </p>
    </proof>
</section>